inline_comments.body:
Done in 2d23491aa. `test_sparse_fp8_nope_matches_fp32_reference` now checks both the ragged kernel and split-K (`num_splits=4`) against an independent FP32 sparse-attention reference, using:

- nonuniform rows of 64 / 0 / 2 / 37 / 300 tokens: an empty row, plus a 2-token row that leaves two of the four splits empty;
- one `-1` slot;
- no sink and a nonzero attention sink;
- `kv_scale` 1.5 and 0.37.

All 8 cases pass on MI355X.

While here, I made the ragged kernel factor the scale the same way split-K does: into the QK scale and the accumulator, instead of dequantizing every tile through FP32 (d3114adad). At GLM-5.3-Flash per-rank shapes, the ragged FP8 path is 3–5% faster than before (0.70–0.82× of BF16 time), and its mean error against FP32 drops by about 30%. Split-K is unchanged.
